<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements for the Phosphor desktop shell.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. The keystore encrypts the keys at rest. That moves them off disk and
  into the heap of a running process, and on macOS a same-user process can attach to another
  with task_for_pid and read that heap. Encryption at rest without a hardened runtime therefore
  moves the key from a file anyone can read to a heap anyone can read, which is not a control.
  The two ship together or neither is real.

  THE ENTITLEMENT THAT IS NOT HERE IS THE POINT. `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` is absent,
  and absent is what matters: it is the entitlement that lets a debugger attach to this process.
  Do not add it, not even temporarily, and not for a "debug build" that might get shipped. The
  three false values below are explicit denials of things the hardened runtime would otherwise
  let this process ask for, written out rather than omitted so that adding one is a visible edit.

  OPEN QUESTION, for whoever runs the first signed build.
  `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` is the one key here that WEAKENS the
  runtime: it allows dylibs signed by anyone to be loaded into the process, which is close to the
  thing the hardened runtime exists to prevent. It is here because the bundle ships its own node
  binary in `externalBin`, and that is the usual reason given for it.

  It may well be unnecessary. Library validation governs dylibs loaded INTO a process, and
  `tauri build` signs the sidecar with the same identity as the app, so a same-team sidecar
  satisfies library validation without this. The payload's three dependencies (viem, zod and the
  MCP SDK) are pure JavaScript with no native addons, so node loads no third-party dylibs either.

  Measured in the tree as it stands (Track A, 2026-09-01): exactly one native binary anywhere in
  node_modules, and it is the Tauri CLI, marked dev and optional in the lockfile, so
  `npm ci --omit=dev` never puts it in the bundle. Of the 107 production packages the payload
  does install, zero have an install script, which is the lockfile's marker for anything that
  compiles or fetches a binding. Zero calls to process.dlopen, createRequire, or a require of a
  .node file in src/ or scripts/. So the bundled node loads system dylibs and nothing else.

  That still cannot be SETTLED without a real signed build, and only the owner holds the
  identity. So: at the first signed build, set this to <false/>, build, and then check the thing
  that would actually break.

  THE CHECK IS NOT "THE APP LAUNCHES". Library validation would bite the CHILD process, not the
  window, and the window comes up first: a shell whose backend never spawned still shows you a
  window. The check is that the backend is alive, and the app has a route that answers exactly
  that with no token and no secret:

      curl 127.0.0.1:4177/api/health

  A 200 with `"ok":true` means node started, bound its port and is serving. If that answers,
  delete this key and this whole comment. If it does not, keep the key and record the failure
  here, so the next person does not repeat the experiment.

  One caveat on the measurement above: it describes today's dependency tree. A native dependency
  added later changes the answer silently, which is the other reason this note stays until
  somebody has run the check.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <false/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <false/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.debugger</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
